   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infection, prevention and control (IPC) is a key public health priority and a critical element in interrupting the transmission of infectious diseases in the Pacific region. 
&#x200d;
Through Australian funding, in 2022-2023, through the Pacific Infection Prevention and Control Network (PICNET) and in collaboration with member countries, SPC conducted a situational analysis of the IPC core components, leading to the first published assessment of IPC programs for Pacific Island countries. This assessment provided a baseline status of the IPC core components, and  identified gaps which was used to inform IPC programme priorities and implementation strategies. 
&#x200d;
The PICTs surveyed ranked well overall, aligning closely to the global average across key indicators. These included the presence of a national program and an annual workplan (67% of PICTs vs. 61.3% globally), the appointment of IPC focal points (87% of PICTs vs. 72.6% globally), and the availability of dedicated IPC budgets (40% in PICTs vs. 48.4% globally). 
&#x200d;
In 2024 through Australian funding, SPC drew on the survey findings to develop a regional IPC dashboard, to enable countries to monitor implementation, and to track progress towards achieving minimum requirements for IPC core components at both national and facility levels.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Vanuatu faces a severe burden of non- communicable diseases (NCDs), accounting for 74% of all deaths. Cardiovascular diseases are the leading cause, responsible for over 30% of all mortalities, while diabetes affects more than 20% of adults, obesity rates are nearly 50% and approximately 40% of adults use tobacco products.
&#x200d;
Vanuatu established an NCD Taskforce in 2019 to address the growing NCD crisis, but it became inactive during the COVID-19 pandemic. In 2023, the Ministry of Health approached the Pacific Community Public Health Division (SPC PHD) for assistance to reactivate the taskforce.
&#x200d;
Through Australian funding, SPC PHD supported the relaunch of the taskforce as the Vanuatu National NCD Multisectoral Committee with a renewed focus on actively monitoring the implementation of Vanuatu&#x2019;s NCD Strategic Plan. SPC PHD supported improved governance, multi-sectoral collaboration and increased activities to implement the Strategic Plan, awareness activities to underscore the impact of NCDs on national development, mobilised communities and built the capacity of staff.
&#x200d;
From July 2024, Australia&#x2019;s partnership with SPC supported tangible progress through the technical working group (TWG) under the Committee, including scoping establishing national maximum nutrient limits, reviewing options for pre-shipment inspection requirements, to support a future ban on some highly processed foods.</narrative>
    </description>
